  "markdown_report": "**Synthesis.** Three starting-point insights guide this early-stage search. (1) Embedding-conditioned retention can suppress stale state selectively. It may prioritize recent coherent behavior without discarding every persistent preference. (2) Final-feature checks fail in all four categories despite zero reported candidate-score discrepancies. Their cause remains unresolved, so temporal conclusions must wait. (3) Training negative-win rates span 0.68–4.29%. These sampled margins establish neither precise top-ten ranking nor validation generalization.\n\nThree literature insights suggest complementary directions. (1) GRU4Rec’s [BPR-max](https://hidasi.eu/assets/pdf/gru4rec_v2_cikm18.pdf) emphasizes competitive negatives. It motivates improving the objective before interpreting easy-negative separation as success. (2) [TedRec](https://github.com/RUCAIBox/TedRec) motivates joint text/ID representations. Its pretrained, transformer-based evidence does not establish benefits for scratch-trained GRU text features. (3) [Interest-drift gates](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.12674) motivate adaptive memory. They do not validate this particular retention gate on the pinned benchmark.\n\n**Framework and candidates.** Organize interventions by their target: execution correctness → memory selection → competitive ranking → item specificity. Correctness is a prerequisite for interpreting every direction. Scores below are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Alignment scores assess hypotheses, not measured improvements.\n\n| Atomic idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessment for each dimension |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. Embedding-conditioned retention before GRU updates | 5/7/8/8/4 | O: focused extension / overlaps GRU reset; P: reusable memory control / uncertain gains; U: targets stale interests and weak recent specificity / cannot establish compatibility; M: explicit padding semantics / temporal benefit unmeasured; D: small cell change / sequential execution cost |\n| B. Regularized BPR-max | 3/7/6/6/3 | O: principled transfer / established method; P: competitive ranking / hardness sensitivity; U: separates close competitors / lacks intent evidence; M: addresses sampled-ranking weakness / cannot fix execution; D: compact loss / sampling care |\n| C. Scratch-trained title/description encoder within GRU4Rec | 4/7/8/5/6 | O: constrained adaptation / familiar fusion; P: sparse-item sharing / limited supervision; U: adds identifiable content / text may omit compatibility; M: frequency-slice hypothesis / cannot fix execution; D: standard pooling / vocabulary and serving work |\n\n**Selected: A, padding-invariant adaptive retention.** At 10% progress, test the smallest architectural extension while preserving the corrected baseline’s loss and optimizer. It explicitly targets two reported user failures: isolated historical interests dominating current needs, and recent repeated behavior receiving insufficient priority. Item/state-conditioned retention may also reduce broad historical associations; exact purpose, redundancy and compatibility remain unobserved.\n\nUse `g=sigmoid(Wx*x+Wh*h+b)`, then `h_new=GRUCell(x,g*h)`. Initialize gate weights to zero and bias to four for near-baseline retention. Padding must preserve every recurrent layer’s state; read the greatest nonpadding index and return zero features for empty histories. This addresses the measured execution-consistency failure through a shared implementation, without asserting its original cause.\n\nUse identical model code in training, ModelView and the embedded predictor. Retain diagnostics and their definitions; rerun temporal probes only after execution checks pass. Preserve four-category routing, fixed seeds, training limits and one checkpoint per category. Select exclusively by official macro validation HR@10; no improvement is established yet.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a coordinate-wise, embedding-conditioned retention gate before each ordinary GRU4Rec recurrent update, with explicit state preservation on padding and one shared scoring implementation.",
    "motivation": "Test whether adaptive memory reduces stale historical influence and gives recent coherent behavior greater priority. First resolve the observed final-feature consistency failures so temporal measurements become interpretable.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the supplied corrected GRU4Rec baseline and retain its loss, optimizer, sampling, splits and routing for this atomic experiment. Initialize gate weights to zero and bias to 4; this approximates retention but is not exactly the baseline. Freeze recurrent state on padding at every layer, mask padded output features, and gather the maximum nonzero sequence index rather than count-1. Empty histories yield zero features while preserving any learned scoring bias. Keep item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), and the actual module's score_candidates consistent, including BxLxK scoring. Use the same architecture definition in the predictor writer and save gate configuration with each checkpoint. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), reporting measured metrics with their definitions. Do not loosen execution tolerances to hide discrepancies. DATA_DIR remains os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; save artifacts beneath the trial directory. Preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs and 200 batches per epoch. Train exactly one independent network for each of Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl; deploy one selected checkpoint per category without averaging or fusion. Use only official macro validation HR@10 for selection. Exact equipment compatibility and purchase satisfaction are not observed labels.",
    "pseudocode": "class RetentionGRULayer(nn.Module):\n    def step(self, x, h, valid):\n        gate = torch.sigmoid(self.gate_x(x) + self.gate_h(h))\n        proposal = self.gru_cell(x, gate * h)\n        return torch.where(valid[:, None], proposal, h)\n\n# Initialize gate_x/gate_h weights to zero and their total bias to 4.\n# In log2feats, initialize all recurrent states to zero.\nfor t in range(sequence.shape[1]):\n    valid = sequence[:, t].ne(0)\n    x = item_emb(sequence[:, t])\n    for layer_index, layer in enumerate(layers):\n        states[layer_index] = layer.step(x, states[layer_index], valid)\n        x = states[layer_index]\n    features[:, t] = torch.where(valid[:, None], x, torch.zeros_like(x))\n\npositions = torch.arange(sequence.shape[1], device=sequence.device)\nlast = torch.where(sequence.ne(0), positions[None, :], -1).amax(dim=1)\nfinal = features[torch.arange(sequence.shape[0], device=sequence.device), last.clamp_min(0)]\nfinal = torch.where(last[:, None].ge(0), final, torch.zeros_like(final))\n# score_candidates and ModelView use this same final-state and scoring path.\n# Train with the supplied baseline objective; save architecture plus state_dict.\n# Check padded/unpadded, empty-history and predictor agreement before temporal probes.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "A focused, testable extension of the supplied baseline that isolates explicit retention before recurrent updates.",
      "negative": "GRUs already contain reset and update gates, and prior recurrent recommenders model interest drift."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "If validated, the gate offers a compact basis for studying selective memory under observed interaction changes.",
      "negative": "No temporal measurements or official validation gains currently support the proposed mechanism."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Requires a small cell-level extension and no additional data or independently fitted model.",
      "negative": "A custom recurrence can reduce throughput and requires careful predictor, padding and diagnostic consistency."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Recurrent Neural Networks with Top-k Gains for Session-based Recommendations",
      "link": "https://hidasi.eu/assets/pdf/gru4rec_v2_cikm18.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces ranking objectives including BPR-max that emphasize high-scoring negative competitors."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish gains for the proposed retention gate or the pinned four-category evaluation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "TedRec",
      "link": "https://github.com/RUCAIBox/TedRec",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies integration of text and collaborative item representations through sequence-level fusion."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its pretrained text features and SASRec backbone do not directly validate scratch-trained text features within GRU4Rec."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Hierarchical recurrent recommendation with interest-drift gates",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.12674",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides related precedent for explicitly modeling changing interests with recurrent gates."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the effectiveness of the proposed embedding-conditioned pre-update retention gate on this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch GRU documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/main/generated/torch.nn.GRU.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Defines recurrent transformations and biases that explain why zero-valued padding inputs alone do not guarantee unchanged state."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Documents execution semantics rather than recommendation accuracy or the cause of the reported discrepancies."
      ]
    }
  ]
